---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const year = new Date().getFullYear();

const meta = [
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Solo Product Designer & Developer' },
  { label: 'Timeline', value: '2024–2026' },
  { label: 'Platform', value: 'iOS (Live on the App Store), Android in development' },
];

const alternatives = [
  {
    method: 'Instagram grid/stories',
    strengths: 'Visual, social proof',
    weaknesses: 'Not searchable, stories expire, no setlist data',
  },
  {
    method: 'Notes app',
    strengths: 'Quick, always available',
    weaknesses: 'No structure, no photos, hard to browse',
  },
  {
    method: 'Spreadsheets',
    strengths: 'Structured, sortable',
    weaknesses: 'Ugly, no emotional connection, no images',
  },
  {
    method: 'Setlist.fm',
    strengths: 'Great setlist data',
    weaknesses: 'Not a personal journal, community-focused not me-focused',
  },
  {
    method: 'Memory alone',
    strengths: 'Zero effort',
    weaknesses: 'Unreliable — details fade fast',
  },
  {
    method: 'Gigz v1',
    strengths: 'Personal, visual, setlists',
    weaknesses: 'Manual entry, no social, no upcoming shows',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Gigz — A Concert Journaling App for Music Fans · Josh Illichmann"
  description="Case study: designing and building Gigz, an iOS concert journaling app for music fans. Year-grouped history, setlist integration, photo personalisation."
>
  <main class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 overflow-x-hidden">
    <div class="space-y-12 py-12 animate-fade-in-up">
      <!-- Back link + profile -->
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <a
          href="/"
          class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-600 hover:text-gray-900 transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2"
        >
          ← BACK
        </a>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <img
            src="/gigz-icon.png"
            alt="Gigz"
            width="40"
            height="40"
            class="rounded-lg"
          />
          <span class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-600 uppercase">Case Study</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Hero -->
      <header class="space-y-6">
        <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-medium font-mono leading-tight">
          Gigz — A Concert Journaling App for Music Fans
        </h1>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          How do you help music fans preserve and revisit their concert history —
          without making it feel like a chore?
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500">
          Version 1 shipped in November 2024. Version 2.0 shipped in September 2026.
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-6 pt-4 text-sm">
          {
            meta.map((item) => (
              <div class="space-y-1">
                <h2 class="font-medium uppercase font-mono text-xs">{item.label}</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-600">{item.value}</p>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </header>

      <!-- Hero image -->
      <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
        <img
          src="/gigz-mock-1.webp"
          alt="Gigz iOS app mockup"
          class="w-full h-auto object-cover"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- The Problem -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">THE PROBLEM</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-4 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Music fans go to dozens, sometimes hundreds of shows over their lifetime.
            But most have no good way to remember them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Think about it — what was that band you saw at The Corner Hotel in 2008?
            Who opened for them? What songs did they play? What did it feel like?
          </p>
          <p>
            Right now, people cobble together workarounds: scrolling through old
            Instagram posts, saving Stories to highlights, keeping scattered notes in
            their phone, or maintaining spreadsheets. None of these are designed for
            the job. They&apos;re fragile, disconnected, and they don&apos;t capture the
            richness of a live music experience.
          </p>
          <p>I wanted to build something that did.</p>
          <p>
            <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">The insight was twofold:</span>{' '}
            this started as a personal pain point — I&apos;m getting older and didn&apos;t
            want to forget the shows from my early years — but the more I looked into
            it, the more I saw a gap. There was no dedicated, thoughtfully designed
            tool for concert journaling that treated the experience with the care it
            deserved.
          </p>
          <p>
            Version 1 shipped and solved that problem — but living with it for a
            year surfaced a second one. Adding a show meant typing in the artist,
            the venue, the date, the setlist, by hand, every time. A diary that
            feels like data entry doesn&apos;t get kept, and that&apos;s exactly
            what people did with it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Research -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">RESEARCH</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-6 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            It started as my own pain point — but I didn&apos;t want to design for an
            audience of one. Before committing to a direction, I did two kinds of
            lightweight research to pressure-test the idea against real people.
          </p>
          <div class="space-y-2">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">1:1 conversations with concert-goers</h3>
            <p>
              I ran informal interviews with friends and acquaintances across the
              spectrum — from once-a-year casuals to people with decades of shows
              behind them. I wasn&apos;t pitching an app; I asked how they remember
              shows today, what they wish they could look back on, and where their
              current workarounds let them down.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-2">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Listening to the wider community</h3>
            <p>
              I spent time in music forums, subreddits, and the App Store reviews of
              adjacent apps to learn the language fans actually use and the gaps they
              complain about — so the product would speak their vocabulary, not mine.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-2">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Living with version 1</h3>
            <p>
              The most valuable research came after launch: watching what real
              users did, and didn&apos;t do, over roughly a year on the App Store
              and in TestFlight.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">What I heard</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
              <li>
                People remember{' '}
                <span class="text-gray-900">feelings and moments</span> — the photo,
                the support act, that one song — far more vividly than dates and
                venues.
              </li>
              <li>
                Almost everyone had a{' '}
                <span class="text-gray-900">&ldquo;lost history&rdquo; regret</span>{' '}
                — early shows they could no longer reconstruct.
              </li>
              <li>
                Existing workarounds (Instagram, Notes, spreadsheets) were described
                as effort, not joy.
              </li>
              <li>
                Setlists came up again and again as the detail that snaps a memory
                back into focus.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">What a year with version 1 showed</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900">Manual entry was the biggest
                drop-off point.</span> Most people logged a few shows and then
                stopped.
              </li>
              <li>
                Shows entered by hand were inconsistent — spellings, venue names
                — which made any kind of stats impossible.
              </li>
              <li>
                People wanted to know about upcoming tours for artists
                they&apos;d already seen.
              </li>
              <li>
                Friends wanted to compare histories with each other, but nobody
                asked for a public profile.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p>
            <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">How it shaped the design:</span>{' '}
            the first round of findings defined the two user types below, pushed
            me toward a visual card-based timeline rather than a list, and made
            photos and setlists first-class features rather than afterthoughts.
            The second round, a year later, became the brief for version 2.0:
            replace manual entry with recognition, make records consistent enough
            to power stats, and add just enough social to compare with friends —
            without ever making the app feel public.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Target Users -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">TARGET USERS</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-6 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>The interviews surfaced two overlapping groups:</p>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="space-y-2">
              <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">The Nostalgic Fan</h3>
              <p>
                Someone who&apos;s been going to gigs for years or decades and wants
                a way to look back. They might remember the headliner but not the
                support act, the venue but not the date. They want to reconstruct
                and preserve their concert history.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="space-y-2">
              <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">The Active Tracker</h3>
              <p>
                Someone who goes to shows regularly and wants to log them as they
                happen. They care about setlists, venues, and building a visual
                record of their music life over time. They also want to know the
                moment a favourite artist announces a new show.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Both groups share a common need: a single, beautiful place to keep their
            concert memories — something that feels more intentional than a notes
            app and more personal than a social feed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Existing Alternatives -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">LANDSCAPE</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-6 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Before designing, I mapped how people were currently solving this
            problem:
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-x-auto -mx-6 px-6">
            <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse min-w-[600px]">
              <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-200">
                  <th class="text-left py-3 pr-4 font-mono uppercase text-xs text-gray-900 font-medium">
                    Method
                  </th>
                  <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 font-mono uppercase text-xs text-gray-900 font-medium">
                    Strengths
                  </th>
                  <th class="text-left py-3 pl-4 font-mono uppercase text-xs text-gray-900 font-medium">
                    Weaknesses
                  </th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  alternatives.map((row) => (
                    <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 align-top">
                      <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-gray-900 font-medium">
                        {row.method}
                      </td>
                      <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-600">{row.strengths}</td>
                      <td class="py-3 pl-4 text-gray-600">{row.weaknesses}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The key gap: nothing combined{' '}
            <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">personal journaling</span>{' '}
            (my photos, my memories) with{' '}
            <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">structured music data</span>{' '}
            (setlists, venues, dates) in a way that was easy to use and beautiful
            to look through.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Design Process -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">DESIGN PROCESS</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-10 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium text-lg">Information architecture</h3>
            <p>
              The core question was: what&apos;s the right mental model for a
              concert journal?
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900">List-based</span> (like a notes app) —
                too utilitarian, no visual delight
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900">Calendar-based</span> — good for active
                trackers but bad for retroactive logging
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900">Card-based timeline</span> — visual,
                scannable, grouped naturally by time
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              I landed on{' '}
              <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
                a card-based timeline grouped by year
              </span>
              . Each card shows the artist, venue, date, and a cover photo —
              giving users an immediate visual anchor for each memory.
            </p>
            <p>
              <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">2.0 evolved this again.</span>{' '}
              The timeline is now a list, with a row of year pills across the
              top — All gigz, 2026, 2025, 2024, and so on — that you scroll
              horizontally and tap to jump straight to a year. The
              grouping-by-year idea survived; the endless scroll didn&apos;t.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium text-lg">The timeline scaling challenge</h3>
            <p>This was the biggest design challenge in the project.</p>
            <p>
              A card-based timeline works beautifully when someone has logged 10–20
              shows. But what happens when a power user has logged 200+ shows
              across 15 years?
            </p>
            <p>
              <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">The problem:</span> If every
              show gets a full-size card regardless of volume, users with rich
              histories would face endless scrolling. But if cards are too
              compressed, you lose the visual impact that makes the journal feel
              personal.
            </p>
            <p>
              <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Version 1&apos;s approach:</span>{' '}
              adaptive card density thresholds based on the number of shows
              within a year grouping — full-size cards at low volume, compact
              cards at medium volume, condensed list entries at high volume. It
              worked, but it was still one long scroll, just with the cards
              shrinking as you went.
            </p>
            <p>
              <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Version 2.0&apos;s approach:</span>{' '}
              stop compressing the scroll and remove it instead. A horizontally
              scrollable row of year pills sits above the list, so someone with
              300 shows across 15 years jumps straight to 2019 instead of
              scrolling past 280 entries to get there. The list itself stays
              simple and consistent, because the pills carry the scale instead
              of the card size.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium text-lg">Setlist integration</h3>
            <p>
              Setlists are a huge part of the concert memory — hearing that one
              song live is often the defining moment. I integrated setlist data so
              users could see (or add) what was played at each show.
            </p>
            <p>
              The challenge here was data quality. Setlist APIs (Setlist.fm) have
              great coverage for major acts but patchy data for smaller venues and
              local scenes. I designed the feature so that:
            </p>
            <ol class="space-y-2 list-decimal pl-5">
              <li>Setlist data auto-populates when available</li>
              <li>Users can manually add or edit setlists when it&apos;s missing</li>
              <li>
                The UI doesn&apos;t feel broken or empty when there&apos;s no
                setlist — it gracefully degrades
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium text-lg">
              Photo management & cover personalisation
            </h3>
            <p>
              In 2.0, photo uploads became a free/premium split: one photo per
              show on a free account, up to 15 on a premium one. The free tier
              isn&apos;t crippled by it — one photo is enough for the
              personalisation moment that matters most: choosing the photo that
              represents the memory. Premium is for people who want a fuller
              photo record of the night.
            </p>
            <p>
              A key personalisation feature, unchanged since version 1: users
              choose which photo becomes the{' '}
              <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">cover image</span> for a
              show in the list. This means two users who attended the same
              concert will have completely different-looking journals. The cover
              photo transforms the entry from &ldquo;I went to this show&rdquo;
              to &ldquo;this is MY memory of this show.&rdquo;
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium text-lg">
              Recognition over recall — rebuilding the Add flow
            </h3>
            <p>
              The single biggest design decision in 2.0 was killing the blank
              form.
            </p>
            <p>
              In version 1, adding a show meant typing everything in from
              scratch: artist, venue, date, setlist. In 2.0, you search for an
              artist and see every show they&apos;ve played in your city. Tap the
              one you were at, and the artist, venue, date, and setlist all come
              with it.
            </p>
            <p>
              <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Why this works:</span>{' '}
              recognition is easier than recall. Seeing &ldquo;Forum Theatre, 14
              March 2019&rdquo; in a list snaps the memory back in a way a blank
              text field never will. And because every show now comes from the
              same structured record instead of a hand-typed guess, the data is
              consistent enough to actually build stats on.
            </p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-3 pt-2">
              <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
                <img
                  src="/gigz-add-flow-1.webp"
                  alt="Gigz Add flow — search for an artist"
                  class="w-full h-auto object-cover"
                />
              </div>
              <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
                <img
                  src="/gigz-add-flow-2.webp"
                  alt="Gigz Add flow — pick the show you were at"
                  class="w-full h-auto object-cover"
                />
              </div>
              <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
                <img
                  src="/gigz-add-flow-3.webp"
                  alt="Gigz Add flow — artist, venue, date, and setlist filled in automatically"
                  class="w-full h-auto object-cover"
                />
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium text-lg">Stats and achievements</h3>
            <p>
              None of this was possible in version 1, because a spreadsheet of
              typed-in text isn&apos;t data you can do anything with. Once every
              show is a real, structured record, the app can show people
              patterns across their own history: shows attended by year, their
              top 5 artists, their top 5 venues. Numbers that only mean
              something because the records behind them are consistent.
            </p>
            <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl overflow-hidden max-w-xs">
              <img
                src="/gigz-stats.png"
                alt="Gigz stats screen"
                class="w-full h-auto object-cover"
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium text-lg">Social, kept quiet</h3>
            <p>
              Version 1 deliberately shipped with no social features at all —
              the journal had to work for an audience of one before it worked
              for two. 2.0 adds social, but under rules designed to stop a
              personal journal turning into a performance:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
              <li>
                Friendships are mutual — there&apos;s no following, only
                agreeing to connect.
              </li>
              <li>
                A friend can see your attended shows, and only the ones
                you&apos;ve chosen to share.
              </li>
              <li>
                Upcoming shows are private by default, with access granted per
                friend.
              </li>
              <li>
                Nothing is public. No discover tab, no follower counts, no
                profile anyone can stumble onto.
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">The reasoning:</span> a
              past show is a memory. A future show is a plan, and plans are more
              sensitive — they say where you&apos;ll be and when. Social in
              Gigz lives where you go looking for it, not on the home screen.
            </p>
            <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl overflow-hidden max-w-xs">
              <img
                src="/gigz-privacy.png"
                alt="Gigz privacy settings screen"
                class="w-full h-auto object-cover"
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium text-lg">Upcoming shows</h3>
            <p>
              2.0 lets you follow artists and see their announced dates in your
              city, so a show moves from upcoming to attended the night it
              happens instead of being typed in after the fact. It closes the
              loop the version 1 research kept surfacing: people wanted to know
              about a tour before it was already over.
            </p>
            <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl overflow-hidden max-w-xs">
              <img
                src="/gigz-upcoming.webp"
                alt="Gigz upcoming shows screen"
                class="w-full h-auto object-cover"
              />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Usability Testing -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">TESTING</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-6 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            I tested Gigz continuously rather than in one big round: informal sessions
            with friends and family watching them add their very first show, and
            several TestFlight builds put in front of real users who lived with the app
            over time.
          </p>
          <div class="space-y-2">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">What I watched for</h3>
            <p>
              Could someone log their first concert without any instruction? Where did
              they hesitate? What did they expect to tap that wasn&apos;t there? I paid
              less attention to what people said and more to where they got stuck.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">What testing changed</h3>
            <p>
              The most important finding was an authentication failure I&apos;d never
              have caught on my own devices:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Original design:</span>{' '}
                Sign in with Apple + a one-time code via SMS.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Problem discovered:</span>{' '}
                some testers on certain carriers silently never received the SMS codes.
                This was a hard blocker — they literally couldn&apos;t get into the
                app, and most wouldn&apos;t have reported it; they&apos;d have just
                left.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Decision:</span> I removed SMS
                OTP entirely and switched to email-based codes. Less flashy, but
                universally reliable.
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">The lesson:</span> don&apos;t let
              a &ldquo;nice to have&rdquo; auth method create a &ldquo;can&apos;t get
              in&rdquo; experience. Beyond auth, testing reinforced two bets: the card
              timeline genuinely delighted people on first scroll, and retroactive
              logging had to be near-frictionless — confirming the design choices the
              rest of this case study is built on.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-2">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Sign-in today</h3>
            <p>
              Sign-in now offers Apple and Google as the primary options, with
              the email one-time code kept on as the reliable fallback rather
              than the default path.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Accessibility -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">ACCESSIBILITY</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-6 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            I built Gigz to be a good iOS citizen from the start, and I treat
            accessibility as an ongoing craft rather than a box to tick.
          </p>
          <div class="space-y-2">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">What I addressed</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Touch targets:</span> sized
                interactive elements to comfortable minimums so the app works
                one-handed and forgives less precise taps.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Dynamic Type:</span> text
                respects the user&apos;s system text-size setting, so the timeline
                stays legible for people who run larger fonts.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-2">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">What I&apos;m still improving</h3>
            <p>
              A full VoiceOver pass, a formal contrast audit across photo-backed cards,
              and a WCAG-style review are next on my list. I&apos;d rather be honest
              that accessibility is a craft I&apos;m actively levelling up than claim a
              finished bill of health. 2.0&apos;s move to iOS 26 and Liquid Glass makes
              the contrast audit more relevant than ever, since translucent materials
              can quietly erode the contrast a photo-backed card relied on.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Key Design Decisions -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">DECISIONS</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-8 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium text-lg">What I built</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
                  Concert journaling with a year-grouped list
                </span>{' '}
                — the core experience, with scrollable year pills for fast
                navigation
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
                  Setlist tracking & integration
                </span>{' '}
                — auto-populated where possible, manually editable where not
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
                  Photo uploads with cover photo personalisation
                </span>{' '}
                — 1 photo free, up to 15 on premium, user-selected cover
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Venue database & search</span>{' '}
                — powered by multiple APIs (MusicBrainz, OpenStreetMap) for
                comprehensive coverage
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Search-and-add show entry</span>{' '}
                — replaces manual input with recognition
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Stats and achievements</span>{' '}
                — built on top of consistent, structured show records
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Friends</span> —
                mutual-only, private-by-default sharing
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
                  Upcoming shows & artist following
                </span>{' '}
                — see announced dates for artists you already follow
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium text-lg">
              What I left out of version 1, and why some of it came back
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Social features</span> —
                No sharing, no friends list, no public profiles in version 1.
                The journaling experience needed to be solid before adding
                social layers, and adding social too early risks turning a
                personal journal into a performance. 2.0 adds social only now
                that the privacy model — mutual friends, private by default —
                exists to answer that risk directly.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
                  Discovery / recommendations
                </span>{' '}
                — still left out. Upcoming shows are for artists you already
                follow, not the app suggesting new ones to you. The app stays
                about your history and your choices, not a feed.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
                  Unlimited photo uploads
                </span>{' '}
                — still not unlimited, but 2.0 turned the flat cap into a tier:
                one photo free, up to 15 on premium, instead of the same limit
                for everyone.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Outcomes & Reflections -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">OUTCOMES</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-8 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium text-lg">What shipped</h3>
            <p>
              Gigz is{' '}
              <a
                href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/gigz-app/id6670392747?itscg=30200&itsct=gigz_web_hero&mttnsubad=6670392747&ct=web_hero"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                class="text-gray-900 font-medium underline"
              >
                live on the App Store
              </a>{' '}
              — a fully functional concert journaling app that lets music fans
              log, revisit, and personalise their concert history.
            </p>
            <p>
              2.0 is a full rebuild: a new data model and backend, the
              search-based Add flow, stats, friends, and upcoming shows, all
              redesigned around iOS 26. One backend holds every visibility
              rule, so when the Android app ships, it inherits the same
              privacy model rather than needing its own version of those
              decisions.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium text-lg">What I learned</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-3 list-disc pl-5">
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
                  Constraints are design tools.
                </span>{' '}
                The photo cap and the decision to cut social features
                weren&apos;t just cost-saving measures — they sharpened the
                product&apos;s identity. Gigz is a personal journal, and every
                decision reinforced that.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
                  Test authentication early and broadly.
                </span>{' '}
                The SMS OTP issue could have been a launch blocker if I
                hadn&apos;t caught it in TestFlight. Auth is infrastructure, but
                it&apos;s also a UX surface.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
                  Scaling UX matters from day one.
                </span>{' '}
                The timeline scaling challenge wasn&apos;t a &ldquo;nice to
                have&rdquo; — it was the difference between an app that works
                for casual users and one that works for everyone. The solution
                changed between versions; the lesson didn&apos;t.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
                  Remove input rather than polish it.
                </span>{' '}
                The best fix for the add-a-show form was to not have one.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
                  Decide what friends can&apos;t see before deciding what they
                  can.
                </span>{' '}
                Privacy defaults are a design decision, not a settings screen.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium text-lg">What&apos;s next</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
              <li>
                On This Day — a memory resurfaced on its anniversary, with a
                shareable Gigz ticket stub designed for that show
              </li>
              <li>More cities (Australian cities only at the moment)</li>
              <li>Android app</li>
              <li>Links to ticket platforms for upcoming shows</li>
              <li>Reminders when a followed artist announces a tour</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

